WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.590
Instructor: All right, so in this video

00:01.590 --> 00:05.850
we're gonna take a peek at some of the internals of Git.

00:05.850 --> 00:08.190
Specifically, I'm gonna show you a command

00:08.190 --> 00:12.810
called git hash-object that I never really use,

00:12.810 --> 00:14.340
except when I'm teaching,

00:14.340 --> 00:17.190
but I do think it explains a lot about how Git works.

00:17.190 --> 00:18.930
Now, remember what we said in the last video,

00:18.930 --> 00:20.790
Git is a key-value data store.

00:20.790 --> 00:22.800
We can give it any sort of information,

00:22.800 --> 00:26.520
including just a simple letter, or a sentence,

00:26.520 --> 00:28.740
let alone files, like we normally do,

00:28.740 --> 00:31.020
and it will give us back a unique key.

00:31.020 --> 00:34.950
So this command, git hash-object, is one way of doing that.

00:34.950 --> 00:36.780
We give it some data

00:36.780 --> 00:39.540
and then it will give us back a unique key,

00:39.540 --> 00:43.260
a corresponding SHA-1 hash, based upon that data.

00:43.260 --> 00:45.120
Remember how a hashing function works,

00:45.120 --> 00:47.820
we don't really care about the internals and how it works,

00:47.820 --> 00:50.550
but we care about the fact that you give it some input,

00:50.550 --> 00:51.750
it doesn't matter the size,

00:51.750 --> 00:56.490
it gives us an output that is always 40 hexadecimal digits.

00:56.490 --> 01:01.170
So this example here, git hash-object and then a file,

01:01.170 --> 01:03.510
is gonna take the content from a file

01:03.510 --> 01:06.360
and then it returns a unique SHA-1 hash

01:06.360 --> 01:08.610
that would be used to store our object,

01:08.610 --> 01:12.510
but it does not actually store anything, in this form.

01:12.510 --> 01:14.610
So this won't store anything for us,

01:14.610 --> 01:17.790
but there's a variant I'll show you where we can tell Git,

01:17.790 --> 01:20.820
actually store this, store this information,

01:20.820 --> 01:24.510
and it will create a new entry in the objects directory.

01:24.510 --> 01:27.360
Remember we talked about it, the objects directory

01:27.360 --> 01:31.170
is where all the information eventually is stored.

01:31.170 --> 01:33.180
All of our files and their contents

01:33.180 --> 01:35.940
and all this stuff we're about to talk about.

01:35.940 --> 01:37.784
So git hash-object on its own

01:37.784 --> 01:40.440
is not gonna be that useful, just like this.

01:40.440 --> 01:41.700
But the next thing I wanna show you

01:41.700 --> 01:44.520
is a somewhat complicated looking variant.

01:44.520 --> 01:47.790
This allows us to hash something that is not a file.

01:47.790 --> 01:50.250
This right here, echo 'hello',

01:50.250 --> 01:52.170
if you're not familiar with the echo command,

01:52.170 --> 01:53.913
if I just echo anything,

01:55.500 --> 01:56.850
it's just returned right back to me,

01:56.850 --> 02:00.570
you know, echo, echo, echo and so on, except only one time.

02:00.570 --> 02:02.490
So we can take that output,

02:02.490 --> 02:04.590
which is just hello in this case,

02:04.590 --> 02:08.160
and then we can pipe it over to git hash-object

02:08.160 --> 02:12.150
and we pass in the --stdin,

02:12.150 --> 02:14.700
that's one of the options that we can pass,

02:14.700 --> 02:17.610
to tell git hash-object that we want it to hash

02:17.610 --> 02:20.400
from the standard in, meaning hash this 'hello'.

02:20.400 --> 02:22.320
It's a very complicated way of telling it,

02:22.320 --> 02:23.940
we don't have a file for you to hash,

02:23.940 --> 02:25.740
just hash this right here.

02:25.740 --> 02:27.390
So let me demonstrate this.

02:27.390 --> 02:30.933
All right, so I'll echo something, we can do "hi"

02:32.850 --> 02:37.850
and then | and then git hash-object and then --stdin.

02:41.070 --> 02:46.070
And notice, it's giving me back a SHA-1 hash, a checksum.

02:46.320 --> 02:48.480
It's 40 characters hexadecimal.

02:48.480 --> 02:52.200
And every time that I call it with that same line,

02:52.200 --> 02:56.550
every time I hash "hi", I should always get the same output.

02:56.550 --> 02:57.810
Remember, it's deterministic.

02:57.810 --> 03:01.590
The same input should always yield the same output.

03:01.590 --> 03:03.810
So I could try this with something else.

03:03.810 --> 03:06.690
What would be another example? How about "hello"?

03:06.690 --> 03:09.030
We get a different output, same length,

03:09.030 --> 03:12.750
40 characters hexadecimal, but very, very different.

03:12.750 --> 03:13.583
So at this point,

03:13.583 --> 03:16.200
I actually haven't even initialized a Git repo.

03:16.200 --> 03:18.510
So the commands that I've showing you there

03:18.510 --> 03:20.214
is like a Git utility,

03:20.214 --> 03:23.640
in the same way that I can run git init anywhere,

03:23.640 --> 03:25.650
my machine just knows git init.

03:25.650 --> 03:28.470
I can run git hash-object anywhere,

03:28.470 --> 03:31.860
but now I'm in a new folder and I'm going to run git init.

03:31.860 --> 03:32.853
It's not a repo.

03:33.870 --> 03:38.370
And then, over in VS Code, I've opened the .git directory.

03:38.370 --> 03:40.860
So I'm showing you the contents here, right?

03:40.860 --> 03:44.580
If I do, ls .git, it now exists.

03:44.580 --> 03:46.680
This is the initial state of .git.

03:46.680 --> 03:49.200
Objects, it has these two folders, info and pack,

03:49.200 --> 03:50.460
but they don't have anything in them

03:50.460 --> 03:52.830
and they don't really matter to us right now.

03:52.830 --> 03:55.620
So objects does not have any of our content in it.

03:55.620 --> 03:58.500
It's just the blank object slate.

03:58.500 --> 04:00.810
All right, so now if I try running one

04:00.810 --> 04:02.220
of these commands again,

04:02.220 --> 04:06.387
like echo "hello" | git hash-object from the standard in,

04:06.387 --> 04:08.130
right, we see that output.

04:08.130 --> 04:09.390
I just wanna make it very clear.

04:09.390 --> 04:11.400
Objects is not changing at all.

04:11.400 --> 04:13.080
We're not storing anything in Git.

04:13.080 --> 04:15.090
This is just saying hello Git.

04:15.090 --> 04:17.370
If you were to store this object,

04:17.370 --> 04:20.490
or this information, just "hello", if you were to store it,

04:20.490 --> 04:23.070
what key would it be stored under?

04:23.070 --> 04:25.560
And that's the output, but it doesn't store it.

04:25.560 --> 04:30.060
However, if we add on this additional option, -w,

04:30.060 --> 04:33.210
I think it's just dash write, w for write,

04:33.210 --> 04:37.350
this is going to tell Git to actually store our object,

04:37.350 --> 04:39.510
actually take this information,

04:39.510 --> 04:42.621
in our case, the string "hello", take that word,

04:42.621 --> 04:46.260
hash it with SHA-1 and then store that.

04:46.260 --> 04:48.420
So after we run this command,

04:48.420 --> 04:50.700
whether we do "hello", or some other string,

04:50.700 --> 04:52.700
I'm gonna do "hello" because it's simple

04:53.550 --> 04:56.040
and I kind of recommend that you follow along,

04:56.040 --> 04:59.070
if you want to, with the same exact string,

04:59.070 --> 05:00.810
so "hello", as well,

05:00.810 --> 05:03.625
because it will make a more consistent experience for you.

05:03.625 --> 05:05.610
'cause this is gonna come back.

05:05.610 --> 05:08.910
All right, so if I just add in the -w,

05:08.910 --> 05:12.450
just wanna verify, prove, nothing in there.

05:12.450 --> 05:15.873
Now, we got this hash as our output, let's hit enter.

05:16.710 --> 05:18.330
We get the same output.

05:18.330 --> 05:22.050
But now, if we go to objects, it might take a moment.

05:22.050 --> 05:23.580
Well, for whatever reason

05:23.580 --> 05:26.010
VS Code is not wanting to refresh and show me.

05:26.010 --> 05:27.930
Let me try refreshing here.

05:27.930 --> 05:29.250
Okay, there we are.

05:29.250 --> 05:32.010
Whew. Okay, so (chuckles) it is indeed here.

05:32.010 --> 05:35.460
There's a new folder, ce.

05:35.460 --> 05:37.020
Now, that's a coincidence.

05:37.020 --> 05:38.430
Let me show you that output again,

05:38.430 --> 05:40.080
of course I, wiped the screen.

05:40.080 --> 05:43.260
But if we run this command,

05:43.260 --> 05:48.260
that hash that we got starts with ce, ce and then 013.

05:49.440 --> 05:53.430
So what do we have? Ce and then what do you know?

05:53.430 --> 05:56.190
0136 and so on.

05:56.190 --> 05:59.130
So there's this entry in the objects folder

05:59.130 --> 06:03.030
and there's a folder inside ce, just two characters,

06:03.030 --> 06:05.610
and then the rest, the 38 remaining digits

06:05.610 --> 06:07.740
are used to name this file.

06:07.740 --> 06:12.150
So this file is the most basic way that Git stores content.

06:12.150 --> 06:15.540
This file actually contains our content

06:15.540 --> 06:17.850
and in this case it was just "hello",

06:17.850 --> 06:19.770
but it's been compressed and encrypted.

06:19.770 --> 06:21.990
So we don't, we can't read it, right?

06:21.990 --> 06:25.350
It's stored as binary in there, but it is in there.

06:25.350 --> 06:28.530
We have successfully stored our first piece of data

06:28.530 --> 06:32.580
in our Git database, the very, very manual way.

06:32.580 --> 06:34.290
We almost never would do this.

06:34.290 --> 06:37.590
I can't imagine really doing this outside of teaching,

06:37.590 --> 06:40.710
but I hope that this explains a tiny bit.

06:40.710 --> 06:43.200
We're not done, there's a lot of pieces of the puzzle here,

06:43.200 --> 06:46.710
but I hope you understand that this git hash-object,

06:46.710 --> 06:50.640
with this annoying syntax, is taking our input "hello",

06:50.640 --> 06:54.038
which always returns the exact same output from SHA-1,

06:54.038 --> 06:55.410
this output,

06:55.410 --> 06:58.410
but we told it to write, with -w previously

06:58.410 --> 07:01.920
and Git took that and it created a file for us.

07:01.920 --> 07:03.960
It's not exactly the same name, right?

07:03.960 --> 07:06.990
It's 38 of 40 digits that are the same.

07:06.990 --> 07:08.460
And then, the first two digits

07:08.460 --> 07:10.260
were used to create the folder.

07:10.260 --> 07:11.430
That's just what Git does.

07:11.430 --> 07:13.115
So the objects directory,

07:13.115 --> 07:16.380
if we had two hashes that happen to start with ce,

07:16.380 --> 07:18.030
they would share this folder.

07:18.030 --> 07:18.863
But then,

07:18.863 --> 07:21.000
if we have another hash that starts with something else,

07:21.000 --> 07:23.490
which I'm about to do here, what should we do?

07:23.490 --> 07:25.230
Let's do "goodbye", as well.

07:25.230 --> 07:29.040
So echo "goodbye", and then pipe that,

07:29.040 --> 07:32.430
basically send the output to hash-object

07:32.430 --> 07:34.620
and tell it to expect standard in.

07:34.620 --> 07:39.030
So I'm not writing this yet, but we get dd7e.

07:39.030 --> 07:42.060
So when I add this -w, I'm telling Git,

07:42.060 --> 07:45.540
all right, store this, store "goodbye".

07:45.540 --> 07:47.550
So it does this all the time, but not with strings,

07:47.550 --> 07:50.490
with files, but I'm telling it store "goodbye".

07:50.490 --> 07:53.280
So we're gonna end up with a folder called dd

07:53.280 --> 07:57.810
and then a file, 7e1, blah, blah, blah, all the way to a6.

07:57.810 --> 08:01.080
And inside, will be a binary, compressed,

08:01.080 --> 08:02.790
encrypted version of "goodbye"

08:02.790 --> 08:05.700
that we won't be able to read, at least not with human eyes.

08:05.700 --> 08:08.200
All right? So I'm gonna add that -w and hit enter.

08:09.480 --> 08:11.280
And of course, we get the same output

08:11.280 --> 08:12.930
because it was the same input.

08:12.930 --> 08:13.800
It's deterministic.

08:13.800 --> 08:15.960
We should always get that same hash,

08:15.960 --> 08:18.690
assuming we give it the same exact input.

08:18.690 --> 08:21.060
So not an extra space, not an extra,

08:21.060 --> 08:23.430
you know, uppercase, lowercase, hidden character.

08:23.430 --> 08:24.633
Exactly the same.

08:25.890 --> 08:28.470
So I'm gonna have to refresh here. There we are.

08:28.470 --> 08:31.410
Now, we have dd and then 7e1.

08:31.410 --> 08:34.680
Okay, so I have two strings, two pieces of text

08:34.680 --> 08:36.900
that I have successfully put into Git.

08:36.900 --> 08:39.150
In the next video, I'll show you how to get them out,

08:39.150 --> 08:42.183
using another Git command that is pretty rarely used.
